==========================================================================================
Part 3: sup-over-x nota -- is the (corrected) bound worse for SMALLER x?
==========================================================================================
  x=0.0: z=20.0, A(z)=0.050869  (larger A(z) = worse/larger bound)
  x=0.5: z=20.5, A(z)=0.049610  (larger A(z) = worse/larger bound)
  x=1.0: z=21.0, A(z)=0.048413  (larger A(z) = worse/larger bound)
  x=3.0: z=23.0, A(z)=0.044148  (larger A(z) = worse/larger bound)
Confirms A(z) DECREASING in z, i.e. INCREASING as x decreases toward 0 --
so x=0 is the worst case for fixed y, and the target's own numerics (fixed
x=1 throughout Sec 3-5) do not probe the worst case over x. The target's
own Sec 8 item 7 discloses this as 'not independently verified across x' --
an honest, correctly-scoped disclosure (NOTA, not a correcao).

